<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prasenajit</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pasenadi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prasenajit, known in Pali as Pasenadi, was king of Kosala in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the time of the Buddha, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Shravasti</a>. Buddhist texts portray him as a lay follower and frequent questioner of the Buddha, and he was linked by marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara of Magadha</a>. He was overthrown by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidudabha">Vidudabha</a> and died outside the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a> while seeking help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prasenajit.jpg" alt="Prasenajit" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. Hargrave Martin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prasenajit_of_Kosala_pays_a_visti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pasenadi, Prasenajit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, was an Aikṣvāka ruler of Kosala, the region of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> or Awadh, who succeeded Sanjaya Mahākosala and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Shravasti</a> in the 6th or 5th century BCE. In his youth he studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>. His first queen was a Magadhan princess, a sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a>, and he gave his own sister Kosala Devi to Bimbisara in marriage.</p><p>His second and chief queen, Vāsavakhattiyā, was a Sākya girl, the daughter of the chief of garland-makers for Mahānāma. With her he had a son, Viḍūḍabha, and a daughter, Princess Vajira, who was later married to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>. As a prominent upāsaka, or lay follower, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a>, he built many Buddhist monasteries for the Buddha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>By the time of Pasenadi, Kosala had become the suzerain of the Kālāma tribal republic, and his realm kept friendly relations with the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> tribe, who lived to the east of his kingdom. A Mallaka named Bandhula, who had been educated in Takṣaśilā, offered his services to the Kauśalya king as a general, so as to keep good relations between the Mallakas and Kosala.</p><p>Bandhula and his wife Mallikā later violated the Abhiseka-Pokkharaṇī, the sacred tank of the Licchavikas, which led to armed hostilities between Kosala and the Licchavikas. Pasenadi afterwards had Bandhula and his sons treacherously murdered. In revenge some Mallakas helped Viḍūḍabha seize the throne of Kosala, and Pasenadi fled the kingdom and died before the gates of Rājagaha, the Magadhan capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> name his successor not as Viḍūḍabha but as Kṣudraka.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pasenadi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He also fought <b>Prasenajit</b> of Kosala, Bimbisara's brother-in-law, for control of Kashi, and Kosala itself was absorbed into Magadha within the following generation or so.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Prasenajit</b> was a lay follower of the Buddha, and the merchant Anathapindika gave the Jetavana grove at Shravasti, where the Buddha spent many rainy seasons.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kingdom of Kosala</a></li><li><blockquote>Mallas were famed as wrestlers and soldiers, and Bandhula, a Malla, served as general to <b>Prasenajit</b> of the Kingdom of Kosala.</blockquote><a href="/state/malla-republic">Malla republic</a></li></ul></section>
